1. No. The provision of water safe for human consumption is a public health matter and responsibility for it lies with the provinces and municipalities.
The government, through emergency health services and the emergency measures organization, provides advice to provinces and municipalities in respect to the following factors concerning wartime contamination of water by radioactive fall-out;
(i)	it recommends, as standards applicable in wartime, levels of contamination beyond which water sources should not be used;
(ii)	it recommends methods of testing water in regard to these standards;
(iii)	it provides information on the techniques for decontaminating water;
(iv)	it is providing an operating manual for water-works engineers for the management of water contaminated by radioactivity in water systems;
(v)	it has provided, through planning pamphlets such as "11 Steps to Survival" recommendations as to the storage of water in homes as an emergency measure;
(vi)	it provides lectures in the emergency health services courses such as those for public health officers, sanitary inspectors and sanitary engineers, dealing with the contamination of food and water and providing advice on techniques for decontaminating such supplies.
Provincial or municipal projects for establishing safe sources of water as part of emergency planning for nuclear war may be eligible for assistance under the financial assistance program. One such proposal accepted under this program was a by pass

pipeline in connection with the Vancouver district of Delta reservoir. This project adds considerably to the reliability of the water supply. The total cost of this project was $1,888 of which the federal share was $1,416.
Additionally, some private organizations had taken steps to provide safe wells for their own use in emergencies.
2. (a) Any source of water in covered storage; (b) water originating in bored well; (c) rivers, since they are capable of selfdecontamination. Relatively soon after attack the level of radioactive contamination of rivers may be so small as to be acceptable.
It should be understood that the emergency of nuclear war may make it necessary to accept somewhat higher levels of radioactive contamination for short periods than would be acceptable over long periods in peacetime.

-I think you have only to refer to the provision under standing order 26, paragraph 6(a), which says:
Not more than one such motion can be made at the same sitting;
We have already had one such motion at this sitting, and it has been ruled out of order. I think this paragraph of standing order 26 takes care of a second motion being introduced.
